    'Fgf3 contributes to inner ear formation and interacts with Fgf8 and Fgf10 during sensory placode development. Global phenotypes center on otology and early patterning, so adult hearing programs often prefer conditional or compound mutant designs. Inner ear restricted Cre drivers isolate postnatal roles after morphogenesis. Knockin alleles modeling human FGF3 related deafness improve translational alignment. Humanized Fgf3 is useful for antibody programs and for human variant knockins expressed from the mouse locus.',
  Fgf4:
    'Fgf4 supports trophectoderm and limb bud outgrowth and is tightly dosage sensitive in early development. Complete loss ends pregnancy early, making conditional or mosaic deletion the only realistic path to later tissues. Limb bud or trophoblast focused strategies answer developmental questions that global KO cannot. Knockin hypomorphs remain valuable when partial FGF4 reduction matches human syndrome dosage. Humanized Fgf4 appears mainly in binder discovery against human FGF4.',
  Fgf5:
    'Fgf5 regulates hair follicle cycling and has additional roles in brain development. The classic long hair phenotype after loss of function made Fgf5 a genetics teaching case and still supports dermatology model design. Conditional skin restricted alleles refine whether systemic loss adds neurological confounders. Knockin alleles help explore human FGF5 variants tied to hair traits. Humanized Fgf5 supports cosmetic and dermatology biologics aimed at human FGF5.',
  Fgf6:
    'Human FGF6 corresponds to mouse Fgf6, involved in FGF receptor tyrosine kinase signaling in morphogenesis and repair. Patterning genes reward spatial and temporal control more than blunt systemic deletion. Global knockout often disrupts early patterning, so conditional deletion of Fgf6 after the critical morphogenetic window is usually required for adult questions. Transgenic overexpression tests gain of morphogen tone when ectopic signaling is the disease hypothesis around Fgf6. A full Fgf6 menu of knockout, conditional knockout, knockin, humanized, and transgenic alleles covers most morphogen program escalations. Antibody programs against human FGF6 should prioritize humanized Fgf6 once developmental viability of the conditional plan is proven. Tissue restricted Cre drivers isolate whether Fgf6 acts from epithelium, mesenchyme, or a discrete organizing center during organogenesis.',
  Fgf7:
    'Fgf7, also called keratinocyte growth factor, promotes epithelial repair in skin, lung, and bladder. Global knockout is often viable with clear epithelial healing deficits after challenge, which suits many injury studies. Conditional epithelial niche deletion still helps when systemic loss complicates interpretation. Transgenic and knockin gain of function models test therapeutic Fgf7 delivery concepts. Humanized Fgf7 supports clinical KGF analog and antibody programs that need human sequence in mouse tissue.',
  Fgf8:
    'Fgf8 is a master organizing FGF for midbrain hindbrain boundary, limb, and cardiac outflow tract development. Null alleles are early lethal, so almost all adult or late organ questions demand floxed Fgf8 with spatially restricted Cre. Hypomorphic knockins historically taught dosage biology and still outperform complete nulls for many craniofacial and cardiac spectra. Humanized Fgf8 is less common but relevant for antibody or binder programs against human FGF8. If your endpoint is adult, start with cKO rather than fighting developmental lethality.',
  Fgf9:
    'Fgf9 drives male sex determination support, lung mesenchyme growth, and joint development, spanning reproductive and pulmonary modeling. Global loss causes lung hypoplasia and male to female sex reversal phenotypes that limit adult male cohorts. Conditional mesenchymal or gonadal deletion is the practical route for postnatal studies. Knockin alleles help explore human FGF9 variants linked to skeletal and reproductive traits. Humanized Fgf9 supports biologics that require human ligand sequence in mouse assays.',
  Fgfr1:
    'Fgfr1 (FGFR1) is a priority signaling hub for mouse modeling because it participates in FGF receptor tyrosine kinase signaling in morphogenesis and repair, which means allele choice quickly decides whether you can score adult pharmacology or only developmental collapse. Point mutant knockins answer activating versus loss of function questions that a clean null cannot, especially when human FGFR1 variants are kinase, GTPase, or scaffolding changes rather than total absence. When the therapeutic modality is sequence selective, design humanized Fgfr1 first and treat mouse only nulls as supporting mechanism tools rather than efficacy workhorses. Model selection should follow whether you need pathway collapse, disease allele mimicry, or human epitope fidelity for the primary endpoint.',
  Fgfr2:
    'Commercial demand for Fgfr2 tracks FGF receptor tyrosine kinase signaling in morphogenesis and repair. Investigators typically compare knockout severity against conditional strategies before committing production scale colonies. Model selection should follow whether you need pathway collapse, disease allele mimicry, or human epitope fidelity for the primary endpoint. Timed deletion after organogenesis finishes separates developmental requirement from adult pathway tone, which is usually what combination drug studies actually need. Humanized Fgfr2 becomes relevant when therapeutic antibodies, degraders, or binders discriminate human FGFR2 from the mouse protein at the epitope level. Conventional knockout clarifies systemic loss when the allele is viable, while floxed Fgfr2 with inducible or tissue Cre is the safer path when developmental compensation or early lethality is likely.',
